## Your Task

- **ID**: TASK-260715-3bdplx
- **Title**: Select the relay language and cross-build toolchain
- **Parent**: STORY-260715-l2i2oo
### Description

Close the unresolved implementation gap for the portable relux-relay target by selecting a language, build system, and reproducible cross-build strategy compatible with rootless Linux and macOS hosts on x86_64 and arm64.
### Scope

In scope: candidate language and runtime footprint; UDP and stdio support; bounded memory; static or self-contained distribution; Linux and macOS x86_64 and arm64 builds; testability; SBOM and license output; reproducibility; team maintenance; protocol-fuzz support; upgrade policy. Out of scope: implementing the relay protocol, remote installation, Apple provider bundling, SSH exec integration, and product behavior.
### Acceptance Criteria

1. A TASK-ID-scoped comparison evaluates at least the viable Swift, Go, and Rust or C-family options against the stated portability, binary, memory, build, testing, security, and maintenance criteria. 2. The selected language, compiler and toolchain versions, dependency policy, target triples, output names, and checksum or manifest format are explicit. 3. A minimal hello-stdio and UDP capability proof or equivalent primary evidence validates every required OS and architecture claim. 4. Tradeoffs and rejected options are recorded, including whether static linking is feasible and what runtime dependencies remain. 5. The decision provides enough detail for the dependent target-shell task to begin without a language or tooling question.

## Instructions

The following instructions have been attached to this task:

### TASK-260715-3bdplx_inputs.md
> Relay toolchain decision inputs

Select the relux-relay implementation language + cross-build toolchain. This portable rootless executable runs on the user EXIT HOST (Linux and macOS, x86_64 and arm64) and speaks the relay protocol over the SSH exec/stdio channel (UDP-over-SSH per ADR-005/006, no SFTP). Per .spec/relay-protocol.md, security-privacy.md (relay supply chain), platform-distribution.md. EVALUATE at least Swift, Go, and Rust/C-family against: portability (static/near-static linking, minimal libc/runtime deps; musl for Linux static), binary size, memory footprint, build reproducibility, testing story, security/supply-chain (SBOM, pinning), and maintenance. AC REQUIRES a minimal hello-stdio + UDP capability PROOF (or equivalent primary evidence) validating EVERY required OS/arch claim — actually cross-build and run the tiny proof where feasible; if a target cannot be cross-built in this environment, record the exact toolchain gap as a blocking item (stop-the-line), do NOT claim unverified support. Decide: language, compiler/toolchain versions, dependency policy, target triples, output names, checksum/manifest format. Output must let the dependent target-shell (1ccx3l) and native-dep seams (1g9cyt) proceed without a tooling question. Consumers: TASK-260715-1ccx3l, 1g9cyt, and the M2 relay build.




